Shift+click on multiple items to select them. The action toolbar will appear above\nyour files in the top-right.\n\ne Toselectallitems, press the key combination Ctrl+a.\n\ne Click Download to begin the download process. Your selected items will be\ndownloaded together in a single zip file.\n\n\nIf you wish to download all items, click the download icon without anything\n\nselected.\nLog in Sign up\nSIZE \"s Details\n234.6 KB\nFolder Properties\n904.6 KB\n\nOwner\n\nTo view a document, simply click the file you want to view. No need for a\nseparate applicaton, it'll open within the web browser.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T16:23:51.379918+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "0b039091-d1fd-4a20-a914-0283395da1b1", "source_url": "https://elections.maryland.gov/voting/documents/Voting System Security Talking Points.pdf", "title": "Voting System Security Talking Points", "section_hierarchy": ["Voting", "Documents"], "page_classification": "document", "chunking_strategy": "document_extraction", "chunk_index": 0, "chunk_total": 3, "word_count": 288, "text": "State of Maryland Maryland’s Voting System – Security Features and Practices Voting System Information • • All voters in Maryland vote on paper ballots. This means that voted ballots can be re-tabulated if there is a need to do so. Maryland’s voting system has ballot scanners, devices for voters with disabilities to mark ballots o privately and independently, and a dedicated computer network at each local board of elections. o Each ballot scanner has a removnaobtle USB memory drive. This memory dr 1 ive stores individual and accumulated results and digital images of all voted ballots . o • The ballot marking device does store or count votes. It simply marks the ballot. The network adds the results from the memory drives and generates official results. Maryland’s voting system has been rigorously tested by a federally accredited testing laboratory • and certified by the U.S. Election Assistance Commission. The certification program tests a voting system against federal penrfeovremrance and security standards. o No part of the certified voting sysnteemve irs connected to the Internet. o Ballot scanners are never connected to the Internet. o Ballot marking devices are ncoenvnere cted to the Internet. o Election results are sent via modem over telephone lines. The system’s certified network is connected to the Internet or the State Board of o Elections’ (SBE) network. A local election official puts the results onto a memory device and takes the memory device to • a computer with access to a primary and secondary secure server. Data are transferred via a virtual private network. This data is encrypted. Results released election night are not official election results. The day after the election, all USB Duties and Responsibilities of Election Officials memory drives are re-uploaded.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T16:23:52.523089+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "77e81e4e-1dbe-42ce-a8d2-66efdffd2968", "source_url": "https://elections.maryland.gov/voting/documents/Voting System Security Talking Points.pdf", "title": "Voting System Security Talking Points", "section_hierarchy": ["Voting", "Documents"], "page_classification": "document", "chunking_strategy": "document_extraction", "chunk_index": 1, "chunk_total": 3, "word_count": 285, "text": "Data are transferred via a virtual private network. This data is encrypted. Results released election night are not official election results. The day after the election, all USB Duties and Responsibilities of Election Officials memory drives are re-uploaded. This process starts the official election results process. • • Election officials test each ballot scanner before each election. They scan pre-marked ballots and compare the results against expected results. If the results match, the unit can be used. • After testing, election officials seal each ballot scanner and store it in a secure location until it is securely moved to a voting location. Each scanner is sealed until it is ready to use. SBE and the local boards of elections have contingency plans if the ballot scanner cannot be used. • Each ballot scanner has a locked and secure place to store voted ballots that cannot be scanned because the ballot scanner is noatl lworking. Election officials audit the voting system results in two ways. SBE uses independent audit software to re-tabulate digital images of voted ballots, and local election officials conduct a risk-limiting audit with manual review of a statistically significant number of paper ballots. The audit results are Security Best Practices compared against the voting system results. • • • All State and local election officials receive regular security training. • Only those election officials that need network access have access. • Election officials only have the access they need to do their jobs. • All employees with network access have had background checks. • All administrative functions in the network and ballot scanners are logged. Data stored on the memory device are encrypted. • Memory devices are assigned to a specific ballot scanner.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T16:23:52.523113+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "0462dd1c-f650-4df3-84d0-2b336666c101", "source_url": "https://elections.maryland.gov/voting/documents/Voting System Security Talking Points.pdf", "title": "Voting System Security Talking Points", "section_hierarchy": ["Voting", "Documents"], "page_classification": "document", "chunking_strategy": "document_extraction", "chunk_index": 2, "chunk_total": 3, "word_count": 116, "text": "Data stored on the memory device are encrypted. • Memory devices are assigned to a specific ballot scanner. If the ballot scanner detects a different memory device, the ballot scanner will not accept ballots. Before the voting system was first used in Maryland, a third party performed a security review on the election night results network. SBE implemented the recommendations. 1 As each voted ballot is scanned, the ballot scanner captures a digital image of both sides of the ballot. This digital 151 West Street, Suite 200  PO Box 6486  Annapolis, MD 21401 image can be retrieved and reviewed later. Local - 410.269.2840  Toll Free - 800.222.8683  MD Relay - 800.735.2258 www.elections.maryland.gov", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T16:23:52.523122+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "8d18f801-89dc-4f98-965e-fb42ad5ecc91", "source_url": "https://elections.maryland.gov/press_room/Voter Registration Security Talking Points.pdf", "title": "Voter Registration Security Talking Points", "section_hierarchy": ["Press Room"], "page_classification": "document", "chunking_strategy": "document_extraction", "chunk_index": 0, "chunk_total": 3, "word_count": 296, "text": "State of Maryland Maryland’s Voter Registration Systems – Security Features and Practices Statewide Voter Registration Database General Information • • Maryland’s voter registration database is centralized, statewide, and owned by the State Board • of Elections (SBE). The local boards of elections access and enter data. A professional web hosting company hosts the database in two separate secure locations. • Access is limited to specific IP addresses. Only IP addresses associated with SBE and the local boards of elections can access the database through a secure network. There are two levels of user authentication. The first level grants access to the platform, and • the second level grants access to the database. All users have unique usernames and passwords for both levels, and one level requires a randomly generated code. • Data are replicated in real time between the two locations. This means both locations have the same data at all times. Security Best Practices The database is backed up nightly. • • • All State and local election officials receive regular security training. • Only those election officials that need access have access. • Only those election officials that need administrative access have this access. • All employees with access to the voter registration database have had background checks. • User authentication policies comply with the State of Maryland’s Information Security Policy. • All transactions – including system configuration and voter data transactions – are logged. Voter registration data are encrypted, and the network uses secure protocols. Online Voter Registration System R egular audits are performed on logs, transactions, and data entry. General Information • • This is a web-based system that allows a user to register to vote, update voter information, and request an absentee ballot. It is an alternate to the paper-based process.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T16:23:53.563856+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "0e08e041-911c-40f3-99b8-a71cfde768fb", "source_url": "https://elections.maryland.gov/press_room/Voter Registration Security Talking Points.pdf", "title": "Voter Registration Security Talking Points", "section_hierarchy": ["Press Room"], "page_classification": "document", "chunking_strategy": "document_extraction", "chunk_index": 1, "chunk_total": 3, "word_count": 292, "text": "Voter registration data are encrypted, and the network uses secure protocols. Online Voter Registration System R egular audits are performed on logs, transactions, and data entry. General Information • • This is a web-based system that allows a user to register to vote, update voter information, and request an absentee ballot. It is an alternate to the paper-based process. To use this system, the user must enter: (1) first and last name; (2) date of birth; (3) zip code of his or her residential address; (4) Maryland driver’s license number or an ID card number issued by Maryland’s Motor Vehicle Administration; (5) the date MVA issued the license or ID • card; and (6) the last four digits of the user’s Social Security number. MVA must verify this information before the user can enter more information. • A professional web hosting company hosts this system in a secure location. This system also • has a back-up location. Data entered into the online voter registration system are stored in an encrypted database. Each night, SBE transfers the data to the statewide voter registration database for processing. A loca l election officniaolt reviews and processes the data in the statewide voter registration o database. A user does create a new voter registration record or update his or her official Security Best Practices voter registration record when he or she uses the online voter registration system. • • • All State and local election officials receive regular security training. All transactions are logged. • Automated reports are generated when unexpected events or data patterns appear, and SBE • staff members review these reports. • Data entered into system are encrypted and stored in an encrypted database. SBE regularly runs security scanning software on the system.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T16:23:53.563878+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "6047f20b-81df-4248-b8d0-cc60e11845e9", "source_url": "https://elections.maryland.gov/press_room/Voter Registration Security Talking Points.pdf", "title": "Voter Registration Security Talking Points", "section_hierarchy": ["Press Room"], "page_classification": "document", "chunking_strategy": "document_extraction", "chunk_index": 2, "chunk_total": 3, "word_count": 81, "text": "All transactions are logged. • Automated reports are generated when unexpected events or data patterns appear, and SBE • staff members review these reports. • Data entered into system are encrypted and stored in an encrypted database. SBE regularly runs security scanning software on the system. Data submitted via this 1s5y1 sWteesmt St raeert,e S ubitea 2c0k0 e dPO u Bpox f 6r4e86q u Aennnatploylis., MD 21401 Local - 410.269.2840  Toll Free - 800.222.8683  MD Relay - 800.735.2258 www.elections.maryland.gov", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T16:23:53.563885+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "d8de5c97-5bd0-4749-ae76-b7f12761e833", "source_url": "https://elections.maryland.gov/laws_and_regs/documents/2023-01 MIB Processing for 2024 Election.pdf", "title": "2023 01 MIB Processing for 2024 Election", "section_hierarchy": ["Laws And Regs", "Documents"], "page_classification": "document", "chunking_strategy": "document_extraction", "chunk_index": 0, "chunk_total": 1, "word_count": 228, "text": "State of Maryland\nSBE Policy 2023-01: MIB Processing for 2024 Election\nAdopted: December 14, 2023\nAnnapolis, Maryland\nThe State Board of Elections issues this policy regarding multiple methods of mail-in ballot delivery\nunder its authority to supervise the conduct of elections and direct the conduct of the local boards\nof elections.\nOn June 1, 2021, House Bill 1048 of the 2021 Maryland General Assembly Legislative session\nwent into effect. Pursuant to section 2(a) of the legislation, at least 60 days before the statewide\nprimary election in 2022 and 2024, each local board of elections shall send the State Board–\napproved mail-in voting ballot application to each eligible voter.\nWhen completing a request for a mail-in ballot on the approved application, the voter has three\ndelivery options from which to select: mail, web delivery, or fax.\nIf a voter chooses more than one method of delivery, one of which includes mail, the policy of the\nState Board for processing the request is to deem the request for a mail delivered ballot.\nTherefore, the State Board directs the local boards of elections to send the voter a ballot by U.S.\nMail when that situation occurs. If a voter chooses more than one method of delivery, which does\nnot include mail, the policy of the State Board for processing the request is to instruct the Local\nBoard to contact the voter.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T16:23:54.563653+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "70a6288c-2384-45d9-b3e3-9c60545cc074", "source_url": "https://elections.maryland.gov/laws_and_regs/documents/Misc-c_SBE Policy 2025-01 Line Management.pdf", "title": "Misc c SBE Policy 2025 01 Line Management", "section_hierarchy": ["Laws And Regs", "Documents"], "page_classification": "document", "chunking_strategy": "document_extraction", "chunk_index": 0, "chunk_total": 1, "word_count": 240, "text": "Maryland State Board of Elections\nSBE Policy 2025-01: Line Management for Voters Who Need Assistance\nThe State Board of Elections issues this policy regarding elderly and disabled voters at polling\nplaces and early voting centers. This policy is issued by SBE under its authority to supervise the\nconduct of elections and direct the conduct of the local boards of elections.\nIn determining locations for an early voting center or polling place, the election director shall\ndetermine whether the location can provide a seating area for elderly and disabled voters in the\nevent of long lines during voting hours. This will be a factor in choosing a location. The election\ndirector shall train election judges to accommodate elderly and disabled voters upon request, in\nthe manner set forth in this policy.\nIf a voter volunteers information to an election judge that they are elderly, disabled, or otherwise\nunable to stand for long periods of time, the election judge shall provide a placeholder card to\nthe voter and another voter who is holding their place in line. The voter requesting assistance\nshall be offered the opportunity to sit down until the placeholder reaches the next stage of the\nvoting process. The voter standing in line will alert the election judges and show them the card\nwhen it is the seated voter’s turn to vote. The cards shall be numbered in pairs to identify the\nvoter who is waiting to vote.\nRevised 01.27.2025", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T16:23:55.622578+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "65a5a467-0c45-43c3-956d-15d23149e0f7", "source_url": "https://elections.maryland.gov/laws_and_regs/documents/Misc-b_SBE Policy 2023-01 List Maintenance Updated Policy.pdf", "title": "Misc b SBE Policy 2023 01 List Maintenance Updated Policy", "section_hierarchy": ["Laws And Regs", "Documents"], "page_classification": "document", "chunking_strategy": "document_extraction", "chunk_index": 0, "chunk_total": 1, "word_count": 205, "text": "Maryland State Board of Elections\nSBE Policy 2025-02: Obituary Policy\nPursuant to Election Law Article §3-101(c)(3)(iii), the State Administrator issues this\npolicy as instructions for the local boards of elections on removing from the statewide\nvoter registration list information about a voter who is no longer eligible due to death.\nThis policy supersedes SBE Policy 2023-02.\nIf a local board of elections receives an obituary identifying a registered voter, a letter\nshall be sent to the address on file, requesting the next of kin sign the letter confirming\ndeath and return the letter to the local board of elections. The only permissible sources\nto be used for an obituary are a newspaper, a printout of a newspaper website with the\nurl, or a printout of the funeral home website with the url. All other source material\nregarding the death of a voter, such as Ancestry.com or Legacy.com, may not be used for\nlist maintenance purposes.\nUpon receipt of a State Board approved obituary for a registered voter, the notice shall be\nforwarded to the local board of elections for processing in accordance with this policy.\nThis commences the verification process required for cancellation. All supporting\ndocuments must be scanned to the voter record.\nRevised 01.27.2025", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T16:23:56.657600+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "e8531bfc-68ad-4508-80dc-d370aa43f820", "source_url": "https://elections.maryland.gov/voting/documents/HowMDVotes_BMD.pdf", "title": "HowMDVotes BMD", "section_hierarchy": ["Voting", "Documents"], "page_classification": "document", "chunking_strategy": "document_extraction", "chunk_index": 0, "chunk_total": 1, "word_count": 147, "text": "1, 2, 3, VOTE\nHow Maryland Votes\n2015.1\nUsing the Ballot Marking Device\n1\nInsert Card\nInsert the ballot activation card given to\nyou by the election worker.\n2\nMake Selections\nMake selections by touching the screen. Press the\nMore button to see all candidates in each contest.\nUse the Previous and Next buttons to navigate\nthrough the ballot contests.\nOr use the key pad and headphones (or your own\naccessibility device) to hear the choices and make\nselections.\n3\nReview, Print & Scan\nReview all selections on the summary screen. If\nchanges are needed, simply press the contest and\nbe redirected to the selection screen for that\ncontest. Once choices are confirmed, press Print\nand remove the voted ballot. To cast your vote,\nscan the ballot into the ballot scanner where it will\ndrop into the secure ballot box.\nMaryland State Board of Elections elections.maryland.gov #MDvotes2016", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T16:23:57.978274+00:00"}
